{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UNeUXrZAZeoPvXxmv72k4b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-23; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13,\n8\t  sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal,\n9\t  \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL `%xx`, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML entities, \"Escape Room\" -> JSON\n10\t  `\\uXXXX`, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\" -> base64url with `=` DROPPED, \"wrong end\"/\"Back at\n11\t  You\" -> the sentence REVERSED, \"A met Z\" -> ATBASH, \"Caesar took a week off\" -> Caesar +7, \"punctuation went for a\n12\t  spin\"/\"Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47, \"Sixteen little symbols ... Dot dot dot\" -> MORSE over HEX, \"Sixteen guests. Five little\n13\t  a's and b's\" -> BACONIAN 5-groups whose values 0-15 are HEX nibbles, \"Three rails ... zigzag\" -> RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0\n14\t  (D18, first true transposition; the prompt's number was the key), \"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes\n15\t  first.\"/\"Little Things\" (D19) -> hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe` BOM, \"Same bar. This time the big one goes first.\"/\"Big\n16\t  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM, \"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\"/\"Under Pressure\"\n17\t  (D21) -> GZIP inside BASE64 (`H4sI...`), \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little inflation.\"/\"Deflated Ego\"\n18\t  (D22) -> ZLIB (`78da`) inside BASE64 (`eNo...`), \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\"/\"Eighty-Five\n19\t  Alive\" (D23) -> ASCII85 in Adobe `<~ ... ~>` delimiters (one layer, plain UTF-8 inside; \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` brackets,\n20\t  \"bigger alphabet\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85). D1-D15 and D23 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n21\t  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = an already-seen codec as the outer layer). D20 was the first day whose twist was a PARAMETER of the\n22\t  previous day's codec (\"Same bar\" = callback, \"big\" = the one knob turned), not a new codec.\n23\t  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n24\t  under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85). Rhythm since D19: NEW family on day\n25\t  N, ONE knob turned on day N+1 (D19 LE -> D20 BE; D21 gzip -> D22 zlib); D23 opened a new family as predicted, so D24 is\n26\t  most likely its knob (another 85-alphabet: RFC1924/git `b85`, Z85, btoa, no delimiters, `z`/`y` shortcuts) or an\n27\t  even bigger alphabet (basE91, base92, base100 emoji, base122, base65536). Read \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" as \"yesterday's\n28\t  codec, one knob turned\"; read \"bigger\"/\"smaller\" as the alphabet SIZE moving.\n29\t- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, in that order so far; 113 x 23 on D23). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n30\t- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s and Mastery ~5-9 s windows (30 s on D1/D12); since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18 had\n31\t  30 s TT / 105 s Mastery again), `run_for_time: true` asks for a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n32\t  60 s regardless (D17 `solver_too_slow`; D21 Mastery 103 x 21 killed). Recent: D21 TT 4.1 s / Mastery 0.34 s solve;\n33\t  D22 TT 2.4 s / Mastery 108 x 22 28.2 s (solve 15.9 s); D23 TT 5.1 s (solve 0.75 s), Mastery 113 x 23 first run\n34\t  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\n35\t\n36\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n37\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n38\t  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n39\t  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n40\t  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n41\t  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n42\t  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n43\t- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n44\t  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n45\t  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n46\t- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n47\t  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n48\t  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n49\t  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n50\t  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n51\t- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n52\t  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n53\t  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n54\t  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n55\t  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n56\t  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n57\t  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n58\t- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n59\t  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n60\t  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n61\t  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n62\t  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n63\t  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n64\t  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n65\t  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n66\t- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n67\t  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n68\t  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n69\t  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n70\t  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n71\t  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n72\t  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n73\t  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n74\t  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n75\t- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n76\t  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n77\t  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n78\t  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n79\t  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n80\t  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n81\t- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n82\t  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n83\t  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n84\t  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n85\t  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n86\t  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n87\t  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n88\t  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n89\t  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n90\t  decoder on the inflated text.\n91\t- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 string `<~...~>`, no newline, no\n92\t  line wraps, no `z` shortcut, 141-143 B (111-113 B of plain UTF-8 -> ceil(n/4)*5 chars + 4 delimiters); Mastery line 23\n93\t  = `<~XXXXXXX~>`, 11 chars on 113/113 lines (5 payload bytes -> 7 chars), EACH fragment with its OWN delimiters, sitting\n94\t  next to base64 (D1, D21, D22) and base32 lines. Ascii85 alphabet `!`..`u` overlaps every other printable codec, so\n95\t  the delimiters are the cue; bare a85 (no `<~`) would collide with ROT47 (D15) text -> slug-gate it. Parse rule (D23\n96\t  solver `_a85`): strip whitespace; `<~...~>` -> `base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else try `a85decode(t)` (handles `z`)\n97\t  then `b85decode` (RFC1924/git) and Z85 (`basenc --z85 -d`, or translate the Z85 alphabet to b85's); sniff\n98\t  compression magic on the bytes, then UTF-8 + slug gate. Mastery: `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` that accepts only a\n99\t  `<~...~>` line -- NEVER rewrite the line in place before `decode_chunked` (Pitfalls, D23). Next knobs on this axis:\n100\t  other 85 alphabets / armors (`xbtoa Begin`, IPv6 RFC1924), bigger alphabets (basE91, base92, base100, base65536).\n101\t- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n102\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n103\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n104\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n105\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n106\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n107\t\n108\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n109\t| What you see | First move |\n110\t|---|---|\n111\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n112\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n113\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n114\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n115\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n116\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n117\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n118\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n119\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n120\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n121\t| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n122\t| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n123\t| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n124\t| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n125\t| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n126\t| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n127\t| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n128\t| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n129\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n130\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n131\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n132\t| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n133\t| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n134\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n135\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n136\t\n137\t## Day by day\n138\t**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n139\t`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\n140\tPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\n141\trot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\n142\tWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\n143\trefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n144\t\n145\t**Days 14-22** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n146\t+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first\n147\tsubmit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug (rank\n148\t3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output\n149\tis a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s budget until `answer()` returned early. D18 the anagram\n150\twas cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; a TT\n151\tstart before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 byte-identical, and D21's\n152\tmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write the GENERIC rule on day N and day N+1's knob is free. D21\n153\tMastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of the carried D20 lane solver -> 60 s kill.\n154\t\n155\t**Day 23: Eighty-Five Alive** (\"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" -- Ascii85, Adobe armor).\n156\t- 143-byte blob, no newline: `<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGmlDJ()6BPDN1G@>N/F...~>` -> plain UTF-8 sentence, one layer.\n157\t  The pun: alphabet size 16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85 (\"got bigger\", \"Eighty-Five\"), \"Adobe\" = the PostScript/PDF Ascii85\n158\t  variant, \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` delimiters. Triage previewed the bytes but routed no codec; the charset alone\n159\t  (`!`-`u`, quotes/backslash-free) plus the `<~`/`~>` brackets is the fingerprint.\n160\t- Single: one command, first submit, start 13:00:14.8Z -> CORRECT 13:00:50.9Z (~36 s; decoded at :45):\n161\t  `python3 -I -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True))\" blob.txt`\n162\t  (`adobe=True` strips the frame and REQUIRES the closing `~>` -- leading `<~` optional; without it `~` raises).\n163\t- Solver: D22 lane solver + `_a85` (a85 -> magic sniff -> UTF-8) as a whole-blob step + `register_free(\"a85\", 23, ...)`.\n164\t  TT (141 B) CORRECT 5.1 s. Mastery 113 x 23: the first submit had all 113 slugs with the right WORDS and the wrong\n165\t  NUMBER (`...-hazel-91` for `-46`, `-09` for `-54`: every digit +5). Cause: `_a85_lines` rewrote line 23 in place to\n166\t  plain `el-46` before `decode_chunked`; the joint free-codec search, which applies SOME free codec to every unclaimed\n167\t  line, gave that plain line a digit-only ROT5. Fix: drop the in-place rewrite for numbered blobs and let the registered\n168\t  free codec consume the raw `<~...~>` line; re-submitted on the same attempt -> CORRECT (solve 1.2 s).\n169\t- Lesson: a Mastery line must reach `decode_chunked` in its ORIGINAL encoding; a new codec enters only through\n170\t  `register_free(name, day, fn)` (or `decode_chunk`). Right words + wrong NN on EVERY set = the last line (the slug tail,\n171\t  today's codec) was mis-assigned, not a GM quirk.\n172\t\n173\t## GM answer conventions learned\n174\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n175\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-23; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n176\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n177\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n178\t\n179\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n180\t- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n181\t  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n182\t  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n183\t- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n184\t  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n185\t  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n186\t  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n187\t  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n188\t- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n189\t  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n190\t  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n191\t- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23, 113/113 sets wrong once): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n192\t  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n193\t  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n194\t  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n195\t- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n196\t  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n197\t  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n198\t  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n199\t  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n200\t- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n201\t  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n202\t- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n203\t  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n204\t- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n205\t  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n206\t- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n207\t  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n208\t  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n209\t  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n210\t- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n211\t  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n212\t  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n213\t- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n214\t  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n215\t  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n216\t- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n217\t  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n218\t  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n219\t- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n220\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n221\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n222\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57a499fe-6ce0-43d7-8cfd-6fceb5b2390a","uuid":"55a3300d-b307-440f-af25-b29ba027b995","timestamp":"2026-10-24T13:21:26.705Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-23; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13,\n  sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal,\n  \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL `%xx`, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML entities, \"Escape Room\" -> JSON\n  `\\uXXXX`, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\" -> base64url with `=` DROPPED, \"wrong end\"/\"Back at\n  You\" -> the sentence REVERSED, \"A met Z\" -> ATBASH, \"Caesar took a week off\" -> Caesar +7, \"punctuation went for a\n  spin\"/\"Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47, \"Sixteen little symbols ... Dot dot dot\" -> MORSE over HEX, \"Sixteen guests. Five little\n  a's and b's\" -> BACONIAN 5-groups whose values 0-15 are HEX nibbles, \"Three rails ... zigzag\" -> RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0\n  (D18, first true transposition; the prompt's number was the key), \"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes\n  first.\"/\"Little Things\" (D19) -> hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe` BOM, \"Same bar. This time the big one goes first.\"/\"Big\n  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM, \"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\"/\"Under Pressure\"\n  (D21) -> GZIP inside BASE64 (`H4sI...`), \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little inflation.\"/\"Deflated Ego\"\n  (D22) -> ZLIB (`78da`) inside BASE64 (`eNo...`), \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\"/\"Eighty-Five\n  Alive\" (D23) -> ASCII85 in Adobe `<~ ... ~>` delimiters (one layer, plain UTF-8 inside; \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` brackets,\n  \"bigger alphabet\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85). D1-D15 and D23 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = an already-seen codec as the outer layer). D20 was the first day whose twist was a PARAMETER of the\n  previous day's codec (\"Same bar\" = callback, \"big\" = the one knob turned), not a new codec.\n  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n  under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85). Rhythm since D19: NEW family on day\n  N, ONE knob turned on day N+1 (D19 LE -> D20 BE; D21 gzip -> D22 zlib); D23 opened a new family as predicted, so D24 is\n  most likely its knob (another 85-alphabet: RFC1924/git `b85`, Z85, btoa, no delimiters, `z`/`y` shortcuts) or an\n  even bigger alphabet (basE91, base92, base100 emoji, base122, base65536). Read \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" as \"yesterday's\n  codec, one knob turned\"; read \"bigger\"/\"smaller\" as the alphabet SIZE moving.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, in that order so far; 113 x 23 on D23). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s and Mastery ~5-9 s windows (30 s on D1/D12); since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18 had\n  30 s TT / 105 s Mastery again), `run_for_time: true` asks for a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n  60 s regardless (D17 `solver_too_slow`; D21 Mastery 103 x 21 killed). Recent: D21 TT 4.1 s / Mastery 0.34 s solve;\n  D22 TT 2.4 s / Mastery 108 x 22 28.2 s (solve 15.9 s); D23 TT 5.1 s (solve 0.75 s), Mastery 113 x 23 first run\n  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n  decoder on the inflated text.\n- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 string `<~...~>`, no newline, no\n  line wraps, no `z` shortcut, 141-143 B (111-113 B of plain UTF-8 -> ceil(n/4)*5 chars + 4 delimiters); Mastery line 23\n  = `<~XXXXXXX~>`, 11 chars on 113/113 lines (5 payload bytes -> 7 chars), EACH fragment with its OWN delimiters, sitting\n  next to base64 (D1, D21, D22) and base32 lines. Ascii85 alphabet `!`..`u` overlaps every other printable codec, so\n  the delimiters are the cue; bare a85 (no `<~`) would collide with ROT47 (D15) text -> slug-gate it. Parse rule (D23\n  solver `_a85`): strip whitespace; `<~...~>` -> `base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else try `a85decode(t)` (handles `z`)\n  then `b85decode` (RFC1924/git) and Z85 (`basenc --z85 -d`, or translate the Z85 alphabet to b85's); sniff\n  compression magic on the bytes, then UTF-8 + slug gate. Mastery: `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` that accepts only a\n  `<~...~>` line -- NEVER rewrite the line in place before `decode_chunked` (Pitfalls, D23). Next knobs on this axis:\n  other 85 alphabets / armors (`xbtoa Begin`, IPv6 RFC1924), bigger alphabets (basE91, base92, base100, base65536).\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\nrot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\nWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\nrefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n\n**Days 14-22** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first\nsubmit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug (rank\n3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output\nis a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s budget until `answer()` returned early. D18 the anagram\nwas cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; a TT\nstart before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 byte-identical, and D21's\nmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write the GENERIC rule on day N and day N+1's knob is free. D21\nMastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of the carried D20 lane solver -> 60 s kill.\n\n**Day 23: Eighty-Five Alive** (\"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" -- Ascii85, Adobe armor).\n- 143-byte blob, no newline: `<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGmlDJ()6BPDN1G@>N/F...~>` -> plain UTF-8 sentence, one layer.\n  The pun: alphabet size 16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85 (\"got bigger\", \"Eighty-Five\"), \"Adobe\" = the PostScript/PDF Ascii85\n  variant, \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` delimiters. Triage previewed the bytes but routed no codec; the charset alone\n  (`!`-`u`, quotes/backslash-free) plus the `<~`/`~>` brackets is the fingerprint.\n- Single: one command, first submit, start 13:00:14.8Z -> CORRECT 13:00:50.9Z (~36 s; decoded at :45):\n  `python3 -I -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True))\" blob.txt`\n  (`adobe=True` strips the frame and REQUIRES the closing `~>` -- leading `<~` optional; without it `~` raises).\n- Solver: D22 lane solver + `_a85` (a85 -> magic sniff -> UTF-8) as a whole-blob step + `register_free(\"a85\", 23, ...)`.\n  TT (141 B) CORRECT 5.1 s. Mastery 113 x 23: the first submit had all 113 slugs with the right WORDS and the wrong\n  NUMBER (`...-hazel-91` for `-46`, `-09` for `-54`: every digit +5). Cause: `_a85_lines` rewrote line 23 in place to\n  plain `el-46` before `decode_chunked`; the joint free-codec search, which applies SOME free codec to every unclaimed\n  line, gave that plain line a digit-only ROT5. Fix: drop the in-place rewrite for numbered blobs and let the registered\n  free codec consume the raw `<~...~>` line; re-submitted on the same attempt -> CORRECT (solve 1.2 s).\n- Lesson: a Mastery line must reach `decode_chunked` in its ORIGINAL encoding; a new codec enters only through\n  `register_free(name, day, fn)` (or `decode_chunk`). Right words + wrong NN on EVERY set = the last line (the slug tail,\n  today's codec) was mis-assigned, not a GM quirk.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-23; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23, 113/113 sets wrong once): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n","numLines":222,"startLine":1,"totalLines":222}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UNeUXrZAZeoPvXxmv72k4b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F94pQSsHVx4gkZjxUt7aiA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Warmups coverage atlas\n2\t\n3\tLane: one `message` question over `blob.txt`. Plaintext is ALWAYS the fixed sentence\n4\t`Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <adj>-<noun>-<noun>-NN`\n5\t(87 chars + slug, ~105-115 bytes). One new keyless codec per day, named by a pun in the title; Mastery line k = day k's\n6\tcodec over a mid-word fragment (`NN<TAB>fragment`). D16-D17 were STACKED: a symbol carrier over HEX (D16 Morse, D17\n7\tBaconian a/b 5-groups whose values 0-15 are the nibbles), both cued by \"Sixteen\". D18 went back to ONE layer and opened\n8\ta new family, TRANSPOSITION: rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0 (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" / \"Off the Rails\");\n9\tthe prompt's number was the key. D19 (\"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes first.\" / \"Little Things\") was\n10\tHEX of the UTF-16LE bytes with a `fffe` BOM: two layers again, the inner one a TEXT ENCODING (byte representation), the\n11\tpun naming code-unit width (\"two bytes\") and byte order (\"little ... first\"). D20 (\"Same bar. This time the big one goes\n12\tfirst.\" / \"Big Entrance\") was the SAME codec with ONE knob turned: hex of UTF-16BE with a `feff` BOM -- a callback pun\n13\t(\"Same bar\") plus the changed parameter (\"big\"); the BOM-aware D19 solver passed it unchanged. Expect more \"same X,\n14\tone knob\" days: the third byte order (middle/PDP-endian), four bytes (UTF-32, prebuilt), no BOM (prebuilt), the whole\n15\ttext as one BIG integer, or a knob on an older day's codec (code-unit escapes byte-swapped, bit order, alphabet).\n16\tD21 (\"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" / \"Under Pressure\") opened a new family, COMPRESSION: GZIP\n17\tinside standard padded BASE64 (`H4sIAAAAAAACA...`, header `1f8b0800 00000000 0203`); Mastery line 21 = each 36-char\n18\tfragment its own gzip member. \"Familiar wrapper\" = an already-seen codec as the OUTER layer. Expect the D19->D20\n19\tpattern again (\"same wrapper, other squeeze\") -- and D22 delivered it: \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little\n20\tinflation.\" / \"Deflated Ego\" = ZLIB (`78da`, level 9) inside std padded BASE64 (`eNo..`/`eNp..`/`eNr..`); Mastery line\n21\t22 = each 20-char fragment its own zlib stream. The D21 solver's `_b64z` (magic sniff incl. `\\x78`) passed it unchanged.\n22\tRHYTHM since D19: day N opens a family, day N+1 turns ONE knob (D19 LE -> D20 BE, D21 gzip -> D22 zlib). D23 opened a\n23\tnew family as predicted, but not a web wrapper: \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" /\n24\t\"Eighty-Five Alive\" = ASCII85 in Adobe `<~ ... ~>` delimiters, ONE layer, plain UTF-8 inside (143 B single, 141 B TT;\n25\tMastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, own delimiters). The pun names the alphabet SIZE (16 -> 32 ->\n26\t64 -> 85 \"got bigger\"), the variant (\"Adobe\" = PostScript/PDF Ascii85) and the armor (\"neighbors\" = `<~` `~>`).\n27\tSo D24 is most likely the KNOB on Ascii85: another 85-alphabet (RFC1924 / git / Python `b85`, ZeroMQ Z85, btoa\n28\t`xbtoa Begin`, IPv6 RFC1924), no delimiters, `z`/`y` shortcuts, line-wrapped armor, or Ascii85 over a seen inner layer\n29\t(PDF ASCII85Decode + FlateDecode is prebuilt); or the alphabet growing again: basE91 / base92 / base94-95 bignum /\n30\tbase100 emoji / base122 / base2048-32768-65536 / 256-glyph byte maps. Expect a \"Same ...\"/\"Bigger still\"/\"Even more\n31\tneighbors\" style callback. Every one of those is keyless -- the charset fingerprint + crib slug gate is the whole job.\n32\tSnapshot 2026-10-23 (after D23; every prebuild gap from the D22 list is built except serialization-wrappers and\n33\tzalgo-combining-strip (1 failed attempt each) and rle-lzw-text-compression / middle-endian-pdp (given up); new base-N\n34\tqueue below).\n35\t\n36\tStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS`, `lib.decode.decode_chunk`/`FREE_CODECS`).\n37\t`prebuilt tgt:<id>` = speculative race module + fixtures from `tests/data/warmups_fixtures/TARGETS.json` (KEPT in\n38\t`.run/prebuild-state.json`). `prebuilt wc:<name>` = encoder/decoder pair in `lib.warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES` (used by\n39\t`infer`/`plaintext`/today-probe; free-text ones also `SPECULATIVE_FREE`). `prebuilt dec:<fn>` = `lib.decode` branch.\n40\t`gap` = nothing in lib (a standard tool may still decode it: chepy 7.x and CyberChef at `tools/npm-cyberchef` are\n41\tinstalled; `.venv` has `base58`, `pycipher`, `Crypto`; NOT installed: base45/base91/pybase62/pybase100/z85/unidecode).\n42\t\n43\t**Crib fact (all keyed rows):** the first 87 plaintext chars are known, so ANY fixed-key letter cipher leaks its key\n44\tfrom the blob alone: Vigenere/Beaufort/Gronsfeld/autokey key = (c - p) / (p - c) / (k - c) over the crib, period =\n45\tsmallest repeat; keyword substitution = the crib map (21 of 26 letters; only f j q y z unseen, slug-only).\n46\t\"Keyless?\" below therefore means \"decodable without outside info\": title pun OR crib.\n47\t\n48\t## Coverage table\n49\t\n50\t| # | concept | status | keyless? | recognition cues | standard decode | D19+ likelihood |\n51\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n52\t| 1 | base64 (std, padded) | seen D1 | yes | `A-Za-z0-9+/`, `=` pad, len%4==0, x1.33 | `base64 -d`; `base64.b64decode` | done (Mastery line 1) |\n53\t| 2 | ROT13 | seen D2 | yes | English word shapes, `Tnzr Znfgre:` | `tr A-Za-z N-ZA-Mn-za-m` | done |\n54\t| 3 | hex (base16) lowercase | seen D3 | yes | `[0-9a-f]` even len, x2 | `xxd -r -p`; `bytes.fromhex` | done |\n55\t| 4 | 8-bit binary, spaced | seen D4 | yes | `[01]{8}` groups | `int(x,2)` | done |\n56\t| 5 | decimal char codes | seen D5 | yes | tokens 32-126, no pad | `chr(int(x))` | done |\n57\t| 6 | octal char codes (3-digit) | seen D6 | yes | tokens `0[4-7][0-7]`/`1[0-7][0-7]` | `int(x,8)` | done |\n58\t| 7 | URL %XX (every byte) | seen D7 | yes | `%[0-9a-f]{2}` x3 len | `urllib.parse.unquote` | done |\n59\t| 8 | HTML decimal entities | seen D8 | yes | `&#\\d+;` only | `html.unescape` | done |\n60\t| 9 | JSON `\\uXXXX` quoted | seen D9 | yes | `\"N...\"` | `json.loads` | done |\n61\t| 10 | base32 RFC4648 padded | seen D10 | yes | `A-Z2-7`, `=` pad, len%8==0, x1.6 | `base32 -d` | done |\n62\t| 11 | base64url unpadded | seen D11 | yes | `-_` and/or len%4 in {2,3}, no `=` | `urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len%4))` | done |\n63\t| 12 | reversed text | seen D12 | yes | `:retsaM emaG`, slug first `NN-...` | `rev`; `[::-1]` | done |\n64\t| 13 | Atbash | seen D13 | yes | `Mrxv dlip`, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` | `tr` mirror; CyberChef Atbash | done |\n65\t| 14 | Caesar +7 | seen D14 | yes | `Upjl dvyr`, best rot-N = 19 | rot-7 | done |\n66\t| 15 | ROT47 | seen D15 | yes | all printable shifted, spaces kept, `|2DE6Ci` | `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O'` | done |\n67\t| 16 | hex variants (UPPER, spaced, `0x`, `:`, `\\x`) | prebuilt wc:hex-upper/-spaced/-0x/-colon, escape\\x | yes | separators/prefixes around hex pairs | `bytes.fromhex(re.sub(...))` | med: cheap \"same family, new dress\" day |\n68\t| 17 | binary variants (7-bit, no spaces) | prebuilt wc:binary7, binary8-nospace | yes | 7-char groups / one 0-1 run | `int(...,2)` | low: D4 did binary |\n69\t| 18 | binary LSB-first / bit-reversed | prebuilt tgt:binary-lsb-first | yes | binary groups decoding to junk MSB-first, every group ENDS `0` (ASCII top bit) | `basenc --base2lsbf -d`; `int(x[::-1],2)` | high: \"little one first\" at bit level (D19 sibling) |\n70\t| 19 | decimal/octal variants (comma, padded, `\\NNN`) | prebuilt wc:decimal-comma*, decimal3, octal, octal-backslash | yes | separators, leading zeros | `int` | low |\n71\t| 20 | charcodes shifted (+N) / other radix (base-4/5/7) | gap | yes (offset from crib) | numeric tokens not in 32-126; 4-digit base-4 | CyberChef From Charcode (base 2-36) | low |\n72\t| 21 | HTML hex entities / named | prebuilt wc:html&#xh;, html&#n;-alnum | yes | `&#x4e;`, `&amp;` | `html.unescape` | low: D8 family |\n73\t| 22 | U+XXXX / `\\U0000XXXX` / CSS `\\4e ` / `%u004e` | prebuilt tgt:unicode-codepoint-notation | yes | `U+004E` tokens; `\\U` 8-hex; `%u` | regex + `chr(int(h,16))`; chepy unicode_to_str | med: \"escape\" family sibling |\n74\t| 23 | base32hex / base32 nopad / lower | prebuilt wc:base32hex, base32-nopad, base32-lower | yes | `0-9A-V`; no `=`; lowercase | `base64.b32hexdecode` | low |\n75\t| 24 | base64 nopad / url padded | prebuilt wc:base64-nopad, base64url | yes | as D1/D11 | pad + decode | low |\n76\t| 25 | base36 | prebuilt wc:base36 | yes | `[0-9a-z]` one case, x~1.55 | `int(t,36).to_bytes` | low |\n77\t| 26 | base45 (RFC 9285) | prebuilt tgt:base45 | yes | `0-9A-Z $%*+-./:`, len%3 in {0,2} | chepy from_base45; CyberChef From Base45 | med: \"QR\"/\"Forty-Five\" pun |\n78\t| 27 | base58 (bitcoin) | prebuilt wc:base58 | yes | no `0OIl+/=`, x~1.37 | `.venv` base58; `basenc --base58 -d` | med: \"Bitcoin\"/\"no zeros\" pun |\n79\t| 28 | base62 | prebuilt tgt:base62 | yes | `[0-9A-Za-z]` only, no pad | chepy from_base62; CyberChef From Base62 | med |\n80\t| 29 | base85 RFC1924 / ascii85 (`<~ ~>`) | seen D23 (Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, plain UTF-8 inside, no `z`, no wraps; Mastery line 23 = `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment; D23 solver `_a85` + `register_free(\"a85\",23)`); RFC1924 `b85`/no-delimiter a85 prebuilt wc:base85, ascii85 | yes | `<~`...`~>` around `!`-`u` (+`z`/`y`) / `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{|}~`, x1.25 | `base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)` (needs the closing `~>`) / `b85decode` | done (Mastery line 23); other 85 alphabets / armors -> row 157 (high: D24 knob) |\n81\t| 30 | Z85 (ZeroMQ) | prebuilt tgt:z85 | yes | `0-9a-zA-Z.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#`, len%5==0 | `basenc --z85 -d` (needs len%4 data); pyzmq z85 | high: the most likely D24 \"other 85\" knob |\n82\t| 31 | basE91 | prebuilt tgt:base91 | yes | 91 printable, no `'-\\` space, x1.23 | chepy