{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgM8eFFmM8pkoad6ffH6m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011uB7gwFY8ePWoJLFfLG3x9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":".venv/bin/python -I - <<'EOF'\np='reference/techniques/warmups.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1, a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"# warmups — technique log (days 1-23; updated after every day)\",\"# warmups — technique log (days 1-24; updated after every day)\")\nrep(\"\"\"  bigger\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85). D1-D15 and D23 one layer;\"\"\",\"\"\"  bigger\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85), \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\" (D24) -> NETSTRINGS\n  `1:N,1:i,1:c,...` (DJB `<len>:<data>,`, every chunk ONE char so far; \"count\" = the length prefix, \"comma\" = the\n  terminator, \"string\" = netstring). D1-D15 and D23-D24 one layer;\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85). Rhythm since D19: NEW family on day\n  N, ONE knob turned on day N+1 (D19 LE -> D20 BE; D21 gzip -> D22 zlib); D23 opened a new family as predicted, so D24 is\n  most likely its knob (another 85-alphabet: RFC1924/git `b85`, Z85, btoa, no delimiters, `z`/`y` shortcuts) or an\n  even bigger alphabet (basE91, base92, base100 emoji, base122, base65536). Read \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" as \"yesterday's\n  codec, one knob turned\"; read \"bigger\"/\"smaller\" as the alphabet SIZE moving.\"\"\",\"\"\"under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85), D24 FRAMING / LENGTH-PREFIXED\n  serialization (netstrings). Rhythm D19-D22: NEW family on day N, ONE knob on N+1 (LE -> BE; gzip -> zlib). D23 -> D24\n  BROKE it (the predicted Ascii85 knob never came; D24 opened framing). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt signals a\n  knob day; otherwise expect a new family -- and the prebuilt lib cannot cover every family, so the first move on an\n  unknown shape is reading the blob's STRUCTURE (here `digits:char,` repeated) and asking what spec it is.\"\"\")\nrep(\"UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, in that order so far; 113 x 23 on D23)\",\"UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, netstrings `1:o,1:n,...`, in that order so far; 118 x 24 on D24)\")\nrep(\"\"\"  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\"\"\",\"\"\"  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\n  D24 TT 4.6 s (solve 1.85 s), Mastery 118 x 24 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try, `deadline: null`.\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering\"\"\",\"\"\"- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,` concatenated, no\n  separators or newline, EVERY len = `1` (444 B for ~111 chars = 4 B per char); the payload's own `,` and `:` appear\n  as data (`1:,,`, `1::,`), and spaces are data (`1: ,`). Mastery line 24 = the same 1-char netstrings over the fragment\n  (`1:o,1:n,1:-,1:3,1:7,`, trailing `,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (D24 solver `_netstr`): cursor loop --\n  `\\\\s*(\\\\d+):` at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end) right after, advance; accept only if the WHOLE text\n  parses (else None, so plain text never matches); `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, fn)` for the Mastery line. Never\n  `split(',')`. The loop already covers len > 1 and mixed lengths; still unbuilt knobs: len in BYTES of UTF-8 vs chars,\n  hex/varint/binary length prefixes, tnetstrings (type tag `,#^!~]}` instead of `,`), bencode (`4:spam`, `l..e`),\n  RESP (`$5\\\\r\\\\nhello\\\\r\\\\n`), HTTP chunked, `index:char` pairs OUT OF ORDER, `count:char` run-length, nested frames.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"| peel finds nothing |\"\"\",\"\"\"| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` doubled up (`1:,,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: `python3 -I -c \"import re,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();o=[];i=0\\\\nwhile i<len(t):\\\\n m=re.compile(r'(\\\\d+):').match(t,i);k=int(m[1]);j=m.end();o.append(t[j:j+k]);assert t[j+k:j+k+1] in (',','');i=j+k+1\\\\nprint(''.join(o))\" blob.txt`. Other framings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag tnetstring, `$5\\\\r\\\\n` RESP, hex-size lines HTTP chunked |\n| peel finds nothing |\"\"\")\n# compress D23 day entry into the summary block, add D24\na=s.index(\"**Days 14-22**\"); b=s.index(\"## GM answer conventions learned\")\ns=s[:a]+\"\"\"**Days 14-23** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`):\nCORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap\nended in a 2-word slug (rank 3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17\na carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s budget until `answer()`\nreturned early. D18 the anagram was cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried\nsolver never routed to it; a TT start before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16`\ncarried D20 byte-identical, and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write the GENERIC rule on day\nN and day N+1's knob is free. D21 Mastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of the carried D20 lane\nsolver -> 60 s kill. D23 (`base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, ~36 s): Mastery 113 x 23 first submit had every slug